<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Big Five (Hawaii)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Big Five were five corporations that dominated the economy and politics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> in the late 19th and early 20th centuries: Castle &amp; Cooke, Alexander &amp; Baldwin, C. Brewer &amp; Co., American Factors and Theo H. Davies &amp; Co. Founded as agents for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations, they also controlled shipping, banking and trade. Their power declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> as unions and the Democratic Party grew stronger.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/big-five-hawaii.jpg" alt="Big Five (Hawaii)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: joanna orpia from QLD, australia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puunene_mill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
